WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:12.100
Welcome to the walkthrough! In this walkthrough, we'll practice spotting relevant costs, then use them for three decisions: a special order, the minimum price for an order, and make or buy.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:28.100
A cost or revenue is relevant only if it passes two tests. It must happen in the future, and it must be different between the choices. Each card names a decision and one item to sort.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:43.300
The first decision: go to a concert, or finish your group project. The one hundred fifty dollar ticket you already bought is a sunk cost. It fails the future test, so it's not relevant.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:54.800
Surf Gear is deciding on a special order. Direct materials of six dollars a towel are bought only if Surf Gear accepts, so they're relevant.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:05.500
Going to college means giving up a full-time salary. That's an opportunity cost. It's in the future and it differs, so it's relevant.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:19.000
Fixed overhead of one hundred thirty-five thousand dollars is a future cost. But the total is the same whether or not Surf Gear accepts the order, so it's not relevant.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:37.400
The other cards follow the same two tests. Book value is sunk. The order has no marketing, so variable marketing doesn't change. The cash from selling the old machine, and tuition, are relevant. A fixed cost you pay either way is not.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:53.000
Now a special order. Surf Gear can make forty-five thousand towels a month and sells thirty thousand at twenty dollars each. A customer offers to buy five thousand towels at eleven dollars each, one time.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:02:04.100
The capacity bars show fifteen thousand towels of spare capacity. The order fits, so accepting gives up no regular sales. There's no opportunity cost.

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:19.700
Count only what changes. The order brings in fifty-five thousand dollars. Variable manufacturing costs are seven dollars and fifty cents a towel, or thirty-seven thousand, five hundred. Operating income rises by seventeen thousand, five hundred.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:31.200
Fixed overhead and fixed marketing stay the same either way, and the order has no marketing cost. So these are crossed out as not relevant.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:42.300
Now drag the order size past the capacity line, to twenty thousand towels. Surf Gear would have to give up five thousand regular towels.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:57.100
Each lost towel has a contribution margin of seven fifty: the twenty dollar price, minus seven fifty of manufacturing cost and five dollars of marketing. That's thirty-seven thousand, five hundred of opportunity cost.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:08.200
Even so, income still rises, by thirty-two thousand, five hundred. Revenue of two hundred twenty thousand covers the variable costs and the lost margin.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:18.100
Drag the offer price down. At nine dollars, income would fall by seven thousand, five hundred, so Surf Gear should reject.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:30.400
The minimum price is nine dollars and thirty-eight cents a towel: seven fifty of variable cost, plus about one eighty-eight of opportunity cost for each order towel.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:43.900
Next, the minimum price for a special order. Mike Products sells its regular product for forty dollars. A customer wants fifty thousand units, with shipping of four dollars a unit.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:57.900
Look closely at indirect manufacturing. It's ten dollars a unit, but sixty percent of it is fixed. That six dollars is paid with or without the order, so it's not relevant.

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:13.500
The order adds direct materials of six dollars, direct labor of three, variable indirect manufacturing of four, and shipping of four. That's seventeen dollars a unit. With spare capacity, the minimum price is seventeen dollars.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:26.200
Now suppose the order pushes out thirty thousand units of regular sales. Each lost unit gives up twenty-seven dollars of contribution margin: forty, minus six, three, and four.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:38.100
That's eight hundred ten thousand dollars of opportunity cost, or sixteen twenty for each order unit. The minimum price rises to thirty-three dollars and twenty cents.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:54.100
The last page is make or buy. Robinson Computers needs fifty-seven hundred units. Making costs one hundred eighty dollars of variable cost plus fifty dollars of fixed cost per unit. A supplier charges two hundred ten.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:05:08.900
Don't compare two hundred thirty with two hundred ten. If Robinson buys, only twenty dollars of that fixed cost goes away. So making has a relevant cost of two hundred dollars a unit.

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:19.600
Making costs one million, one hundred forty thousand. Buying costs one million, one hundred ninety-seven thousand. Making is cheaper by fifty-seven thousand dollars.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:30.700
Turn on the fixed cost you pay either way. The same one hundred seventy-one thousand lands on both bars, so the gap doesn't change.

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:43.800
Now suppose the freed capacity could earn seventy thousand dollars. Making gives up that income, so it's an opportunity cost of making. Buying is now cheaper, by thirteen thousand.

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:56.500
Before deciding, weigh the qualitative factors: quality, supplier reliability, employee morale, and logistics. They don't show up in the numbers, but they can outweigh a small cost difference.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:08.800
To practice, press New numbers on any page. You get a fresh version of the same problem. Answers show as question marks until you work them out.

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:14.500
Work out the answer, type it in, and press Check.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:19.900
Correct! The hidden amounts appear, along with the worked answer.

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:26.800
Try each page yourself, then press New numbers to practice. Thanks for watching.
